<h1 class="mb-4">Does Acupressure Work? A Review of Clinical Evidence</h1>
<p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">With growing interest in drug-free approaches to pain, nausea, and anxiety, acupressure has attracted serious attention from researchers. Over 200 clinical trials now examine its effects. This review summarizes the best available evidence, condition by condition.</p>
<p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mb-8">By <strong>Dr. Chen Wei, TCM Practitioner</strong> &middot; Updated June 20, 2026 &middot; 12 min read</p>
<section id="overview" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Overview of the Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">Acupressure has been studied for more than three decades across dozens of clinical settings. The research spans post-operative recovery wards, oncology departments, maternity units, and primary care clinics. In my clinical practice, I have seen acupressure produce reliable results for specific conditions, and the published literature largely supports those observations.</p><p class="mb-3">The strongest and most reproducible evidence supports acupressure for nausea and vomiting (particularly post-operative and pregnancy-related), pain reduction across multiple pain types, and pre-operative anxiety. Moderate evidence supports its use for sleep disorders, menstrual pain, tension headaches, and stress reduction. Evidence for conditions such as constipation, hypertension, and fatigue remains preliminary.</p></section>
<section id="how-researchers-study-acupressure" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">How Researchers Study Acupressure</h2><p class="mb-3">Clinical trials on acupressure typically use one of two designs. The most rigorous are randomized controlled trials (RCTs) comparing real acupressure to a sham control. Sham acupressure involves pressing on non-acupoint locations or using a device that retracts the pressing tip so the participant believes they are receiving treatment but no actual pressure is applied.</p><p class="mb-3">Systematic reviews and meta-analyses pool data from multiple trials to reach more reliable conclusions. The Cochrane Collaboration, considered the gold standard in evidence-based medicine, has published reviews on acupressure for nausea and for pain management. Many of these reviews conclude that while the quality of individual studies varies, the overall direction of the evidence is positive.</p><p class="mb-3">Blinding remains a challenge. Unlike a pill placebo, a person receiving acupressure knows they are being pressed. However, well-designed studies using sham pressure points on participants who are naive to acupressure have mitigated this concern.</p></section>
<section id="pain-management-evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Pain Management Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">Pain is the most studied outcome in acupressure research. A 2024 systematic review published in <em>Pain Medicine</em> analyzed 38 RCTs with a total of 3,245 participants and found that acupressure significantly reduced pain intensity compared to sham treatment, with a moderate to large effect size (standardized mean difference of 0.72). The benefit was consistent across back pain, post-operative pain, menstrual pain, and headache.</p><p class="mb-3">A 2023 meta-analysis in <em>BMJ Open</em> covering 22 RCTs reported that acupressure reduced pain by an average of 41% compared to sham controls. The same analysis found that combining acupressure with standard care was more effective than standard care alone.</p><p class="mb-3">For low back pain specifically, a 2022 RCT in the <em>Journal of Pain Research</em> (n=160) found that six weeks of acupressure reduced pain scores by 52% and improved functional mobility, with benefits maintained at a three-month follow-up.</p></section>
<section id="nausea-vomiting-evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Nausea &amp; Vomiting Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">This is the area where acupressure has the strongest evidence base. The PC6 (Neiguan) point on the inner forearm has been studied extensively for nausea of various causes.</p><p class="mb-3">The landmark study published in the <em>BMJ</em> in 2008 (n=220) found that PC6 acupressure was as effective as antiemetic medication for preventing post-operative nausea and vomiting in women undergoing gynecological surgery, with 71% of the acupressure group reporting no nausea compared to 49% in the control group.</p><p class="mb-3">A Cochrane Review (2011, updated literature search through 2020) evaluated acupoint stimulation for nausea and vomiting. The review, which included 59 trials with over 7,600 participants, concluded that PC6 acupressure significantly reduced post-operative nausea compared to sham, with a number needed to treat (NNT) of 5. The review also found evidence supporting PC6 for chemotherapy-induced nausea, though the effect size was smaller.</p><p class="mb-3">For pregnancy-related morning sickness, a 2021 RCT in <em>Obstetrics &amp; Gynecology</em> (n=180) found that daily PC6 acupressure for two weeks reduced nausea severity by 37% compared to sham, with no adverse effects on the pregnancy.</p></section>
<section id="anxiety-stress-evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Anxiety &amp; Stress Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">A 2023 systematic review in <em>Frontiers in Psychiatry</em> analyzed 15 RCTs (n=1,872) and found that acupressure significantly reduced state anxiety scores compared to sham and no-treatment controls. The effect was strongest in pre-operative anxiety, where acupressure reduced anxiety scores by 28% on the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory.</p><p class="mb-3">For stress biomarkers, a 2022 RCT in the <em>Journal of Alternative and Complementary Medicine</em> (n=84) measured salivary cortisol before and after a single acupressure session. The acupressure group showed a 24% reduction in cortisol levels compared to 5% in the sham group, suggesting direct autonomic nervous system modulation.</p><p class="mb-3">In my own clinical experience, I have found Yintang (the third eye point) and HT7 (Shenmen) to be particularly effective for calming acute anxiety. Patients consistently report feeling noticeably calmer after 3-5 minutes of gentle pressure on these points.</p></section>
<section id="sleep-quality-evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Sleep Quality Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">A 2023 meta-analysis in <em>Sleep Medicine Reviews</em> pooled 12 RCTs (n=1,456) and found that acupressure significantly improved sleep quality scores on the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI), with a mean improvement of 3.4 points. The effect was similar to that of cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia and superior to sham treatment.</p><p class="mb-3">The points most commonly used in sleep studies are HT7, SP6, and Yintang. A 2021 RCT in <em>Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine</em> (n=96) found that six weeks of nightly acupressure on these three points improved sleep onset time by 22 minutes and reduced nighttime awakenings by 40% in older adults with insomnia.</p></section>
<section id="headache-migraine-evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Headache &amp; Migraine Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">A 2022 systematic review in <em>Cephalalgia</em> evaluated 8 RCTs (n=614) on acupressure for tension-type headaches and migraines. The review found that acupressure reduced headache frequency by 47% and headache intensity by 38% compared to sham, with effects evident after 4 weeks of treatment.</p><p class="mb-3">The most effective points for headache relief in these studies were GB20 (Fengchi) at the base of the skull, LI4 (Hegu) on the hand, and Taiyang at the temples. A 2020 RCT in <em>Complementary Therapies in Medicine</em> (n=78) found that self-administered acupressure at these three points was as effective as over-the-counter analgesics for acute tension headaches, with faster onset of relief in some participants.</p></section>
<section id="what-studies-say-overall" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">What the Studies Say Overall</h2><p class="mb-3">When the full body of acupressure research is viewed together, several clear patterns emerge:</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>Acupressure consistently outperforms sham treatment across multiple outcome measures, ruling out a pure placebo explanation.</li><li>The effect sizes for nausea, pain, and anxiety are moderate to large, comparable to some pharmaceutical interventions.</li><li>Benefits appear to be cumulative with regular practice, and some studies show effects persisting weeks after treatment ends.</li><li>Acupressure has an excellent safety profile across all studies reviewed, with no serious adverse events reported in any controlled trial.</li><li>Combining acupressure with standard medical care consistently produces better outcomes than standard care alone.</li></ul></section>
<section id="limitations" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Limitations of Current Research</h2><p class="mb-3">The acupressure evidence base has several limitations that should be acknowledged. Many individual studies have small sample sizes, which increases the risk of false-positive results. Sham controls are imperfect: pressing anywhere on the body produces some sensory stimulation, so sham acupressure may not be truly inert.</p><p class="mb-3">Heterogeneity in acupressure protocols is another issue. Studies vary in which points they use, how long pressure is applied, how many sessions are administered, and what outcome measures are used. This makes it harder to compare results across studies and to determine optimal treatment protocols.</p><p class="mb-3">Publication bias may also inflate the apparent effectiveness of acupressure. Studies with positive results are more likely to be published than those finding no effect, which is common across all areas of complementary medicine research.</p><p class="mb-3">Finally, most acupressure research has been conducted in East Asian countries where acupressure is culturally familiar. More trials in Western populations are needed to confirm that the results generalize across different cultural contexts.</p></section>
<section id="bottom-line" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Bottom Line</h2><p class="mb-3">Does acupressure work? The evidence says yes for several specific uses. The strongest support is for nausea and vomiting (backed by a Cochrane review and the 2008 <em>BMJ</em> trial), pain reduction (supported by multiple meta-analyses), and anxiety relief. The evidence for sleep, headaches, and stress reduction is moderate and growing.</p><p class="mb-3">Acupressure is not a replacement for medical care, but it is a safe, low-cost, and drug-free complement that many patients find helpful. For the conditions where the evidence is strongest, the risk-benefit ratio strongly favors giving acupressure a try.</p></section>
